"Pennsylvania lawmakers have introduced House Bill 2879, one of the most restrictive online gambling proposals tabled in a mature US market. Reported on 27 October 2026, the bill is led by Representative Tarik Khan with eight co-sponsors and is under review by the House Gaming Oversight Committee. Its stated aim is to curb aggressive marketing and address gambling addiction, and it reaches well beyond the incremental responsible gaming tweaks that regulators in other states have favoured.",
"On the player side, the bill would allow only two account deposits in any 24-hour period and set a $5,000 deposit ceiling every 180 days unless the operator completes a formal affordability check. Credit cards, government assistance cards and gambling gift cards would be banned as funding sources. Every player would have to set loss limits, and operators would be required to delay any request to raise them. For players aged 21 to 25 the bill goes further, with a maximum $2 wager per casino play and a mandatory 30-day cooling-off period after losses of $750 in a day or $2,500 in a month.",
"The operator-facing provisions are where the commercial impact would be greatest. Microbets and proposition wagers would be removed from the market, and push notifications, sign-up bonuses, free bets and VIP rewards would be prohibited. Marketing could not use terms such as 'risk-free', and gambling advertising would be barred during live sports broadcasts and at certain hours. Taken together, these measures would dismantle much of the acquisition and retention toolkit on which licensed operators in Pennsylvania currently rely.",
"The affordability mechanism deserves particular attention. A $5,000 limit that can be lifted only by a 'formal affordability check' would import a concept familiar from the UK into a US regulatory setting that has so far avoided source-of-funds style assessments. Operators would need to decide what documentation they can lawfully request, how long checks take, and how to reconcile them with privacy and data-protection obligations. Counsel should expect the design of that process to attract as much debate as the headline limit.",
"Legislative reality tempers the immediate risk. The session ends on 30 November 2026, the bill is at committee stage, and measures this far-reaching often serve to set an agenda rather than to pass in their introduced form. Even so, introduction signals the direction of travel on product restrictions, and parts of the bill, such as credit card funding bans or limits on promotional mechanics, could resurface in narrower vehicles or in Pennsylvania Gaming Control Board rulemaking.",
"For licensed operators and platform providers, the practical response is scenario planning rather than system changes. Commercial teams should model the revenue effect of removing bonuses, microbets and push notifications, while compliance teams should map where existing deposit, loss-limit and cooling-off tooling could be configured to the bill's parameters. Specialist US gaming counsel can assist with legislative monitoring, comment submissions and contingency planning for the 2027 session if the bill lapses.",
